# ABOUT PERL

Perl is a general-purpose programming language originally developed for
text manipulation and now used for a wide range of tasks including
system administration, web development, network programming, GUI
development, and more.

The language is intended to be practical (easy to use, efficient,
complete) rather than beautiful (tiny, elegant, minimal).  Its major
features are that it's easy to use, supports both procedural and
object-oriented (OO) programming, has powerful built-in support for text
processing, and has one of the world's most impressive collections of
third-party modules.

For an introduction to the language's features, see pod/perlintro.pod.

For a discussion of the upstream 5.40.2 release, see pod/perldelta.pod.

There are also many Perl books available, covering a wide variety of topics,
from various publishers.  See pod/perlbook.pod for more information.


# ABOUT PERL5-MT

`perl5-mt` is a standalone fork of Perl 5.40.2.  It asks a simple, practical
question: what happens if Perl can use all the cores on a modern machine,
keep its memory footprint honest, and still run thirty years of Perl 5 and
CPAN code without making you rewrite your life's work?

Stock Perl's `ithreads` model works by cloning the entire interpreter tree for
every thread, which is faithful to Perl's semantics and unkind to your RAM
and startup time.  `perl5-mt` takes a different path.  Your program compiles
and runs its `BEGIN` and `INIT` blocks as usual, and then seals the compiled
interpreter (op trees, subs, stashes, and regex programs) into a read-only
boot image protected by hardware memory protection keys or `mprotect`.
Worker threads share that image in the same address space and keep only
their own pads, globs, and stash overlays private.

Because the real world is full of CPAN XS modules that were never written
with threads in mind, `perl5-mt` splits XS into two tiers.  Modules built
thread-safe run directly in-process in every worker.  Everything else runs
transparently in out-of-process XS compartments over shared-memory rings,
so an uncooperative C library cannot corrupt your workers, and if it crashes,
the compartment takes the fall and your workers get a clean exception.

Around that core, `perl5-mt` adds cooperative stackful fibers (`MT::Fiber`),
kernel-async I/O (`:async` over `io_uring` or `kqueue`), channels, cells,
and shared hashes (`MT::Channel`, `MT::Cell`, `MT::SharedHash`), fail-fast
supervision (`MT::Fault`, `MT::Supervisor`), SwissTable hashes behind `HV`,
opt-in packed typed arrays (`MT::Array`), dual-gated SIMD string and regex
kernels, cross-host bit-identical binary64 and decimal128 numerics
(`core-math`, `MT::Decimal`), an extensible hardware offload layer
(`accel/`), and VM call/specialisation optimizations that preserve
`B::Deparse` output.

Every acceleration path carries a scalar twin and a runtime kill-switch
(`PERL_FORCE_SCALAR=1`, `PERL_ACCEL=off`, `PERL_MT_NO_SPECIALIZE=1`), so
when something looks suspicious you can always ask the boring code path for
a second opinion.

Here is what `MT::Pool`, `MT::Fiber`, and `MT::Array` look like together:

    use v5.40;
    use MT::Pool;
    use MT::Fiber;
    use MT::Array;

    sub score_batch ($n) {
        my @f = map {
            my $s = $_;
            MT::Fiber::schedule(sub { my $t = 0; $t += ($_ + $s) * 0.25 for 1..$n; $t });
        } 1 .. 4;
        MT::Fiber::run();
        my $sum = 0; $sum += MT::Fiber::join($_) for @f;
        return $sum;
    }

    my $pool = MT::Pool->new(workers => 4);
    my @sums = $pool->map(\&score_batch, 100, 200, 300, 400);
    $pool->shutdown;
    say "total: ", MT::Array::sum(MT::Array::packed("NV", @sums));


# MEASURED NUMBERS

Numbers are better than promises.  The table below summarizes reproducible
runs of `./perl -I. -Ilib tools/bench-suite.pl` on two hardware-verified
systems: an Apple M2 Max (macOS arm64, 12 cores, `NEON` + `Accelerate`) and
an Intel Core Ultra 7 265K (Linux x86_64, 20 cores, `AVX2`).  Every numeric
and SIMD row is checked for bit-for-bit parity against its scalar reference.

| Workload | Apple M2 Max (`macOS arm64`) | Core Ultra 7 265K (`Linux x86_64`) |
|---|---|---|
| 36 SIMD string / UTF-8 / regex kernels | 1.93x geomean, 17.52x peak | 3.01x geomean, 17.34x peak |
| `MT::Array` 131k `sum` / `axpy` / `dot_i8` vs pure Perl | 11.9x / 13.2x / 587.1x | 1.2x / 10.5x / 563.8x |
| SwissTable `HV` 100k `iterate` / `miss_lookup` | 1.19x / 1.14x | 1.23x / 1.19x |
| Frozen VM `static_method` / `mixed_hot_loop` | 1.49x / 1.20x | 1.60x / 1.21x |
| 8-worker `MT::Pool` startup (32 MiB image) vs `ithreads` | 8.95 ms (4.86x faster) | 12.81 ms (10.01x faster) |
| `MT::Fiber` switch & 8W x 4F scaling | 340.1 ns (6.22x at 8W) | 184.2 ns (5.96x at 8W) |
| `MT::PDLBridge` 524k `f64` 4W shm vs 1T scalar | 35.96x (4.50 ms) | 21.66x (4.10 ms) |
| `MT::Channel` cross-worker ping-pong RTT | 1.31 us (762k trips/s) | 1.48 us (676k trips/s) |

Building with `-Dusepgo -Dusenative` (`make pgo`) on Apple M2 Max adds a
further 1.07x to 1.51x across the suite, bringing 8-worker pool throughput
from 2,939.6 to 3,614.3 ops/s and 32-fiber throughput from 43,732 to
61,906 items/s, while keeping the exact same cross-host SHA-256 numeric digest
(`3cc524ed2f4a700c9d6bfe6047d8d6cecfaea84c3e7fe74747ad4f9e70d0271b`) across
macOS `arm64` (`clang`) and Linux `x86_64` (`gcc`).

Full tables, methodology, caveats, and reproduction commands live in
[MT_BENCH.md](MT_BENCH.md).


# PLATFORM STATUS

To keep expectations honest:

- **Hardware-verified today**: `macOS arm64` (Apple Silicon, `NEON` +
  `Accelerate` + `kqueue`) and `Linux x86_64` (`AVX2`, `io_uring`, memory
  protection keys), both passing the full Perl core regression suite,
  `t/mt/*.t`, `krt`, `accel`, and cross-host `t/mt/num_digest.t` parity.
  Earlier runs were also hardware-verified on `Linux ppc64le` (POWER8,
  `VSX`).
- **Software-complete, community testing welcome**: `Linux ppc64le`,
  `Linux arm64`, `Linux s390x`, `macOS x86_64`, **AIX 7.x** (`ppc64`
  XCOFF/TOC), **IBM i PASE** (`ppc64`), **z/OS USS** (`s390x` LP64 and
  XPLINK-64, Enhanced ASCII and EBCDIC), and additional offload backends
  (`Apple Metal`, `ANE`, `Mesa OpenCL` on AMD/Intel GPUs, `Intel NPU`,
  `AMD NPU`, `POWER NX`, and `IBM z16/Telum II NNPA`).  The native `krt/`,
  `mt_ctx_*.c`, `hints/`, and `accel/` backends are in the tree; if you have
  access to any of that hardware, build logs and test reports are warmly
  welcome (see [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)).


